{:edb/db-before-t 3757 :edb/db-after-t 3758 :edb/tx-data [[13194139537070 50 #inst "2026-10-18T03:49:39.537Z" 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186304814 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nI. $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n\nIII. $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186304814 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186304814 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186304814 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186304815 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186304815 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n- **I.** $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- **II.** $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- **III.** ${x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186304815 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)