efore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186304968 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186304968 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **II.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **III.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186304968 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186304968 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186304969 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186304969 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **II.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **III.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186304969 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186304969 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186305090 1071 "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise function defined on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform \n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186305090 1071 "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise function defined on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform \n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $f(t)$ contains a discontinuity for some value of $t>0,$ we have $F(s)=0$ for all $s>a.$\n- **II.** $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$\n- **III.** The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186305090 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Smoothness has no bearing on whether the Laplace transform vanishes.\nA function of exponential order always has a well-defined Laplace transform for $s>a$, and there is no reason for that transform to be identically zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The smoothness theorem states that for $f(t)$ of exponential order, the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f}(s)\n$$\n\nis infinitely differentiable at every point in its region of convergence, and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}(s).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The Laplace transform $F(s)$ of a function $f(t)$ is always infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence, even if $f(t)$ is non-smooth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186305090 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $f(t)$ contains a discontinuity for some value of $t>0,$ we have $F(s)=0$ for all $s>a.$” is false. Smoothness has no bearing on whether the Laplace transform vanishes.\nA function of exponential order always has a well-defined Laplace transform for $s>a$, and there is no reason for that transform to be identically zero.\n\n- Statement “$F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$” is true. The smoothness theorem states that for $f(t)$ of exponential order, the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f}(s)\n$$\n\nis infinitely differentiable at every point in its region of convergence, and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}(s).\n$$\n\n- Statement “The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$” is true. The Laplace transform $F(s)$ of a function $f(t)$ is always infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence, even if $f(t)$ is non-smooth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is “$F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$”; “The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$” only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308376 1071 "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308376 1071 "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=2.0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308376 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$ falls *outside* of the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$ falls *outside* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ falls *within* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308376 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$ falls *outside* of the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$ falls *outside* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ falls *within* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.4$; $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308436 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308436 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3y+x$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1$\n- **III.** $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+5$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308436 1074 "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+