Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308467 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-7{y}_{n}+(-7{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$” is false.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(7Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-7Δx|}{|2+7Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-7Δx| & <|2+7Δx| \\\\ (2-7Δx)^{2} & <(2+7Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$”; “The method is absolutely stable” only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308493 1071 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308493 1071 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$\n- **II.** $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308493 1074 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$ corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$ corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308493 1074 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308534 1071 "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{