ation $y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$ is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}-2.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′})$ is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={y}^{′}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation $x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$ is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$; $x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308566 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}-2.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={y}^{′}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308567 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308567 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n- **I.** $y=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})$\n- **II.** $y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{2}=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308567 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation $y=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation $y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$ is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{2}=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{x}$ is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. It cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308567 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. It cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308570 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308570 