uation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308575 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308575 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **II.** ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **III.** The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308575 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-3(3{e}^{3x})+2({e}^{3x}) & =0 \\\\ 8 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x}-3(2{e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308575 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-3(3{e}^{3x})+2({e}^{3x}) & =0 \\\\ 8 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x}-3(2{e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308585 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308585 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.95$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308585 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.95,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.95)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.95}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.95}| \\\\  & =0.5128…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit 