en applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{5}{8}| \\\\  & =0.625<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.6)| & =|1-1.6+\\frac{(1.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{17}{25}| \\\\  & =0.68<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.2)| & =|1-2.2+\\frac{(2.2)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{61}{50}| \\\\  & =1.22>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.5)| & =|1-3.5+\\frac{(3.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{29}{8}| \\\\  & =3.625>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the Modified Euler Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308669 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{5}{8}| \\\\  & =0.625<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.6)| & =|1-1.6+\\frac{(1.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{17}{25}| \\\\  & =0.68<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.2)| & =|1-2.2+\\frac{(2.2)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{61}{50}| \\\\  & =1.22>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.5)| & =|1-3.5+\\frac{(3.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{29}{8}| \\\\  & =3.625>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.5$; $\\lambda Δx=1.6$ only.\n\nNote that the stability region of the Modified Euler Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308676 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308676 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308676 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$ corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{5}.$ So, statement $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6}$ corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$ So, statement $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$ corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308676 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{5}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308677 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308677 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308677 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{6}=2(Δx)^{5+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7}$ corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{4}=0.4(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{5}=6(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8}$ corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8}$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308677 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{6}=2(Δx)^{5+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{4}=0.4(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{5}=6(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308678 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308678 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308678 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{3}=0.3(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6}$ corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{5}=9(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}$ corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6}$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308678 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{3}=0.3(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{5}=9(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308679 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186308679 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186308679 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo