n turn. \n\n- Statement $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$ is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5$ is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323674 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{5} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=5=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323702 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323702 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n- **I.** $A={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0}$\n- **II.** $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=\\frac{1}{2}y+2}$\n- **III.** $C={0}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323702 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. There is only one vector in ${R}^{2}$ that satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0,$ namely, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩.$ So, $A={0}$, which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x=\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ since $0\\ne \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+2.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩,$ which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323702 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. There is only one vector in ${R}^{2}$ that satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0,$ namely, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩.$ So, $A={0}$, which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x=\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ since $0\\ne \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+2.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩,$ which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $A={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0}$; $C={0}$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323707 1071 "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323707 1071 "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n- **I.** $f(x)\\in S,$ where $f(x)=2x.$\n- **II.** For every $p(x),q(x)\\in S,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in S.$\n- **III.** The set is closed under addition.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323707 1074 "Let's verify the statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$ However, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(2x)=1\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2x\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $S$ such that their sum is *not* in $S.$ For example, let $p(x)=x+{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=x-{x}^{2}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $S$ since $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(q(x))=2\\ge 2.$ However,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x)+q(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡((x+{x}^{2})+(x-{x}^{2}))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(2x)=1≱2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323707 1074 "Let's verify the statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\in S,$ where $f(x)=2x.$” is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$ However, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(2x)=1\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2x\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement “For every $p(x),q(x)\\in S,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in S.$” is false. There exist functions in $S$ such that their sum is *not* in $S.$ For example, let $p(x)=x+{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=x-{x}^{2}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $S$ since $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(q(x))=2\\ge 2.$ However,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x)+q(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡((x+{x}^{2})+(x-{x}^{2}))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(2x)=1≱2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement “The set is closed under addition” is false. Since statement “For every $p(x),q(x)\\in S,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in S.$” is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323736 1071 "![](images/c5/c51c5a4de562e5a3301ea76afc6af57b459acd4c46e5c65d54afc226d883a137.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323736 1071 "![](images/c5/c51c5a4de562e5a3301ea76afc6af57b459acd4c46e5c65d54afc226d883a137.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-3,0]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-3,1)$\n- **III.** $x\\in [0,3)$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323736 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,0]$ and $x\\in [0,3).$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/00/00a80dde24ca012e0282362224c8b63764f5e149a8bfcda9c76ff4eb958fa93a.png)\n\n \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,1)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea54918e2ef4310b58e9cf8a0c9b1f9757a404d97dbbfd9e5b037af2193f38c.png)\n\n \n\nHence, the correct answer is '$x\\in (-3,0]$ and $x\\in [0,3)$ only''." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323736 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,0]$ and $x\\in [0,3).$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/00/00a80dde24