t “$Ax=0$ has the unique  solution $⟨0,0⟩$” is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$⟨5,-4⟩$ is a solution to $Ax=0$” is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement “$Ax=0$ has the unique  solution $⟨0,0⟩$” is true." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323750 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323771 1071 "To which of the following sets do all the real numbers belong?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323771 1071 "To which of the following sets do all the real numbers belong?\n- **I.** ${Z}^{-}$\n- **II.** $N$\n- **III.** $Q$\n- **IV.** ${Q}^{-}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, 