numbers as, for instance, $2$ don't belong to ${Z}^{-},$ so ${Z}^{-}$ does *not* contain the positive integer numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$N$ (natural numbers)”; ${R}^{+}$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323843 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323843 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n- **II.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- **III.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323843 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(2v)=2(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=2(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $-2$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323843 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement “$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$” is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n- Statement “If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(2v)=2(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=2(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $-2$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement “$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$” is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$”; “$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$” only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323858 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323858 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $5v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323858 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=3v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ 