sponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement “An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$” is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Statement “If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $5v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(5v)=5Av=5(-3v)=-3(5v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(5v)=-3(5v),$ and $5v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $5v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{3}.$”; “If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $5v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$” only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323865 1071 "![](images/1e/1eccd6630b69558f8a86f8336e9f0e49f37ffb29acf4a3c8f58be34346b4c93c.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323865 1071 "![](images/1e/1eccd6630b69558f8a86f8336e9f0e49f37ffb29acf4a3c8f58be34346b4c93c.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [-2,0]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-3,-2]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-3,