 belong to ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323901 1074 "First, we note that $\\frac{72}{9}=8$ is a natural number, so it belongs to $N.$ \n\nConsequently, $\\frac{72}{9}$ also belongs to ${Q}^{+}$ and $R.$\n\nSince $\\frac{72}{9}$ is positive, it does not belong to ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$N$ (natural numbers)”; ${Q}^{+}$; “$R$ (real numbers)” only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323902 1071 "To which of the following sets does the number $-\\sqrt{81}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323902 1071 "To which of the following sets does the number $-\\sqrt{81}$ belong?\n- **I.** $Z$\n- **II.** ${Z}^{-}$\n- **III.** ${Z}_{0}^{-}$\n- **IV.** $R$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323902 1074 "Note that $-\\sqrt{81}=-9.$ Therefore $-\\sqrt{81}$ is a negative integer. So it's in the sets $Z,$ ${Z}_{0}^{-},$ and ${Z}^{-}.$ \n\nMoreover, since every integer is also a real number, $-\\sqrt{81}$ also belongs to $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323902 1074 "Note that $-\\sqrt{81}=-9.$ Therefore $-\\sqrt{81}$ is a negative integer. So it's in the sets $Z,$ ${Z}_{0}^{-},$ and ${Z}^{-}.$ \n\nMoreover, since every integer is also a real number, $-\\sqrt{81}$ also belongs to $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$Z$ (integers)”; ${Z}^{-}$; ${Z}_{0}^{-}$; “$R$ (real numbers)”." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323908 1071 "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${Q}_{0}^{+}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323908 1071 "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${Q}_{0}^{+}?$\n- **I.** $0$\n- **II.** $\\sqrt{27}$\n- **III.** $-\\sqrt{4}$\n- **IV.** $\\frac{1}{9}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323908 1074 "Remember that ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and $0.$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}$ is not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}=-2$ is a negative number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $\\frac{1}{9}$ is a rational number because it is the ratio of two integers, and moreover, it is a positive rational number. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{9}$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323908 1074 "Remember that ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and $0.$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}$ is not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}=-2$ is a negative number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $\\frac{1}{9}$ is a rational number because it is the ratio of two integers, and moreover, it is a positive rational number. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{9}$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The number $0$”; $\\frac{1}{9}$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323917 1071 "The function   $f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(4,8).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323917 1071 "The function   $f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(4,8).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(4,8)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)>0$\n- **III.** The point $(4,8)$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323917 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4x-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y)=-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2y)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}^{2}+4x & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(4,8),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4,8)=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(4,8)>0$ is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(4,8),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(4,8)=det({f}^{″}(4,8))=(-32)\\cdot (-2)-{4}^{2}=48>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)>0$ is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)=({f}^{″}(4,8))_{11}=-32<0.$\n\n- Statement “The point $(4,8)$ is a saddle point of $f$” is false. Since $H(4,8)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)<0,$ the point $(4,8)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $H(4,8)>0$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323917 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4x-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y)=-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2y)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}^{2}+4x & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(4,8),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4,8)=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(4,8),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(4,8)=det({f}^{″}(4,8))=(-32)\\cdot (-2)-{4}^{2}=48>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)=({f}^{″}(4,8))_{11}=-32<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(4,8)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)<0,$ the point $(4,8)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323919 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323919 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n- **I.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$\n- **II.** $(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$\n- **III.** ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda =0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186323919 1074 "Since $B$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$ could  be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation $(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$ could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda =0$ **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$; $(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$ only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186323919 1074 "Since $B$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could  be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186326671 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186326671 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $E$ is **not**  a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(E)=-2$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186326671 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 8\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(40-42)-3(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{12}{3})+4(\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{12}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186326671 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 8\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(40-42)-3(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{12}{3})+4(\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{12}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$E$ is a singular matrix” is true." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186326673 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186326673 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Q$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $Q$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(Q)=-5$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186326673 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 