nt I is true. Indedd, $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Simplifying the expression $(6a-9b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6a-9b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ (6a)\\cdot a-(9b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a)-9(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a) & =9(b\\cdot a) \\\\ a\\cdot a & =\\frac{3}{2}(b\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. If $b\\cdot a=-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6a-9b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a)-9(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a)-9(-4) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a & =-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut this is a contradiction since $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186326833 1071 "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186326833 1071 "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$\n- **II.** $[T]_{B}=[T]_{C}^{-1}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186326833 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/9b/9ba3d1f0eebdfc82c40f36bdbe16896ca15516b623c695b72c331861955329bc.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebrai