}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution. Moreover, ${x}_{3}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent.\nTherefore, from ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{3}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{3}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{3}}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement “${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$” is false. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{4}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{4}$” is false. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{2}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}$” only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186329743 1071 "Let $W$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186329743 1071 "Let $W$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={2}^{x}.$\n- **II.** For any $f(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in W.$\n- **III.** The set is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186329743 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={2}^{x}\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple *not* in $W.$ For example, let $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $f(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $f(0)=1.$ However,\n\n$$\n2\\cdot f(0)=2\\cdot {2}^{0}=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nHence, $k\\cdot f(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186329743 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement “$q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={2}^{x}.$” is true. Indeed, $q(x)={2}^{x}\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “For any $f(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in W.$” is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple *not* in $W.$ For example, let $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $f(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $f(0)=1.$ However,\n\n$$\n2\\cdot f(0)=2\\cdot {2}^{0}=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nHence, $k\\cdot f(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement “The set is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since statement “For any $f(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in W.$” is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={2}^{x}.$” only." 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186439088 1077 "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186439088 1077 "I" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186439089 1077 "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186439089 1077 "II" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186439090 1077 "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186439090 1077 "III" 13194139537070 17592186576411 false] [17592186439091 1077 "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$" 13194139537070 17592186576411 true] [17592186439091 1077 "I" 13194139537070 175